Facade Design Pattern:
---------------------
	Facade pattern provides a simple,unified interface to a complex subsystem.
	It hides internal complexity and makes the system easy to use and loosely coupled.
	
Real-Time Use Case:

Online Shopping-Order placement:
------------------------------
	when user places an order , multiple subsystems are involved.
->inventory check
->order management
->shipping arrangment
->Notification service

	->without Facade pattern, a client must talk to all subsystems directly(tight coupling)
	
	
Without Facade(Problem)
-----------------------

class OrderClient
{
 public static void main(String args[])
 {
	InventoryService inventory=new InventoryService();
	PaymentService payment=new PaymentService();
	ShippingService shipping=new ShippingService();
	NotificationService notification=new NotificationService();
	
	inventory.checkStock("IPhone");
	payment.makePayment("CreditCard",1000);
	shipping.shipProduct("IPhone");
	notification.sendNotification();
 }
}

Problems:
--------
Client knows too much.
Hard to maintain.
Difficult to change subsystem logic.

With Facade pattern(Solution)
-----------------------------

subsystems classes:

class InventoryService
{
 public void checkStock(String product)
 {
  System.out.println("Checking stock for :"+product);
  }
}

class PaymentService
{
 public void makePayment(String type,double amt)
  {
	System.out.println("Processing :"+type+" payment $"+amt);
 }
}

class ShippingService
{
 public void shipProduct(String product)
  {
	System.out.println("Shipping product:"+product);
 }
}

class NotificationService
{
 public void sendNotification(String message)
  {
   System.out.println("Notification sent:+"message);
   }
}

Facade class:(Simplied class)
-----------------------------

class OrderFacade
{
	private InventoryService inventory;
	private PaymentService payment;
	private ShippingService shipping;
	private NotificationService notification;
	
	OrderFacade()
	{
	this.inventory=new InventoryService();
	this.payment=new PaymentService();
	this.shipping=new ShippingService();
	this.notification=new NotificationService();
	}
	
	public void placeOrder(String product,String type,double amt)
	{
	inventory.checkStock(product);
	payment.makePayment(type,amt);
	shipping.shipProduct(product);
	notification.sendNotification("Order placed Successfully:+"product);
	}
}

client code(simple and clean)
---------------------------

class Client
{
	public static void main(String args[])
	{
		OrderFacade facade=new OrderFacade();
		facade.placeOrder("IPhone","CreditCard",1000);
	}
}


Output:

Checking stock for iPhone
Processing Credit Card payment of $1200
Shipping product: iPhone
Notification sent: Order placed successfully for iPhone


Benefits of Facade

✅ Red offers a single entry point
✅ Reduces coupling
✅ Improves readability & maintainability
✅ Makes legacy systems easier to use

Note:
Facade pattern provides a simplified interface to complex subsystems, improving usability and reducing coupling.



	
